  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1111 AM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nWater vapor satellite imagery and RAP upper air analysis at midday \nreveal a pair of 500-mb troughs dominate the synoptic pattern\nover the CONUS, with one centered over the upper Midwest and \nanother upstream over the Intermountain West. At the surface, \nsouthwest KS resides between a ~997-mb low over the upper \nMidwest and weak high pressure over the southern plains, \nsupporting light and variable winds across our area. Throughout \nthe remainder of the day today, DNVA in the wake of the eastern \ntrough should keep skies mostly clear and winds will remain \nlight and variable, yielding a very pleasant fall day with \nafternoon temperatures peaking in the upper 50s to low 60s. \n\nLater tonight, short range ensembles agree the western trough will \nadvance eastward onto the central plains by 12Z Thursday. As forcing \nfor ascent increases over southwest KS ahead of this feature, light \nto moderate rain showers will cross the CO/KS border around \nmidnight, and spread eastward with time favoring areas along and \nsouth of KS-96. REFS members along with the HRRR imply this activity \nwill have some convective elements, suggesting the precipitation \ntotals will be much more hit-or-miss instead of a broad swath of \nroughly even rainfall amounts. As such, QPF forecasts are all over \nthe place from ensemble members, resulting in a wide range of \npotential outcomes for specific locations. Precipitation totals \ncould range from zero/just a trace up to 1\"+ by the time it exits \nour area late afternoon/early evening Thursday.\n\nFriday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles depict \nan abnormal degree of variance between solutions regarding the \noverall synoptic evolution over the CONUS. That said, the general \nindication is a prolonged dry stretch for southwest KS is ahead, \nwith little to no opportunities for precipitation and mild afternoon \ntemperatures with highs in the 50s and 60s through mid next week.",
